# Job control in the shell (roadmap os-server.md P11; XCU 2.11): ^Z stops
# cat, jobs lists it, fg brings it back to read a line; cat & stops at its
# read in the background (SIGTTIN), bg would only stop it again, fg finishes
# it; ^C ends a job and not the shell, and drops a line at the prompt.
# (Pids: init 1, its children for the eight terminals 2 to 9, then the
# jobs and echo, a program. A cat in the background may stop before the
# next prompt, which then says so too: the lines below in order, others
# between them.)
# (The keys after ^C wait a moment for cat's end, as after Ctrl+Break in
# break.test.)
program: sh
match: contains
keys: cat\n{ctrl-z}jobs\nfg\nhello\n{ctrl-d}echo $?\n
keys: cat &\n{wait 20}jobs\nfg %1\nworld\n{ctrl-d}
keys: cat\n{ctrl-c}{wait 20}echo $?\nabc{ctrl-c}echo still here\n
---
rx286, test build
/mnt/rx286$ cat
^Z
[1] Stopped  cat
/mnt/rx286$ jobs
[1] Stopped  cat
/mnt/rx286$ fg
cat
hello
hello
/mnt/rx286$ echo $?
0
/mnt/rx286$ cat &
[1] 12
/mnt/rx286$ jobs
[1] Stopped  cat
/mnt/rx286$ fg %1
cat
world
world
/mnt/rx286$ cat
^C
/mnt/rx286$ echo $?
130
/mnt/rx286$ abc^C
/mnt/rx286$ echo still here
still here
/mnt/rx286$
